          "title": "Expanding a single bracket",
          "subtitle": "Multiply every term inside the bracket by the term outside.",
          "index": "3",
          "total": "9",
          "progress": "30"
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "title": "The distributive law",
          "flow": [
            {
              "kind": "para",
              "text": "A bracket is a shorthand for repeated addition. When a term sits outside a bracket, it multiplies **every** term inside — not just the first one. This is the distributive law, and it is the single rule behind every expansion you will meet this year."
            },
            {
              "kind": "equation",
              "math": "a(b+c)=ab+ac",
              "ref": "1"
            },
            {
              "kind": "para",
              "text": "Read it left to right to expand; read it right to left to factorise. The sign in front of a term travels with it, so $-2(3x-5)$ becomes $-6x+10$, not $-6x-10$."
            },
            {
              "kind": "subheading",
              "text": "Where students go wrong"
            },
            {
              "kind": "para",
              "text": "The two most common errors are multiplying only the first term, and losing the sign on the second term. Both are caught by substituting a value such as $x=1$ back into the original and the expanded form."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "figure": "graph",
          "title": "y = 3(x+4) and y = 3x+12 are the same line",
          "caption": "The expanded and factorised forms plot identically — a graph is a check on your algebra.",
          "domain": {
            "xMin": -6,
            "xMax": 4,
            "yMin": -4,
            "yMax": 16
          },
          "grid": "shown",
          "aspect": "stretch",
          "objects": [
            {
              "type": "function",
              "f": "3*(x+4)",
              "label": "y = 3(x+4)"
            },
            {
              "type": "points",
              "from": "table",
              "rows": [
                [
                  "A",
                  -4,
                  0
                ],
                [
                  "B",
                  0,
                  12
                ]
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "workedExample",
          "eyebrow": "Worked example",
          "title": "Expand $-2(3x-5)$",
          "steps": [
            {
              "t": "$-2 \\times 3x = -6x$",
              "note": "Multiply the outside term by the FIRST term inside."
            },
            {
              "t": "$-2 \\times (-5) = +10$",
              "note": "Negative times negative is positive — this is the sign students lose."
            },
            {
              "t": "$-2(3x-5) = -6x+10$",
              "note": "Write the two products as a sum."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "selfCheck",
          "eyebrow": "Self-check",
          "prompt": "Expand $5(2x-3)$.",
          "hint": "Multiply BOTH terms inside by 5, and keep the minus with the 3.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "desc": "Multiply the first term inside by 5.",
              "work": "$5 \\times 2x = 10x$"
            },
            {
              "desc": "Multiply the second term inside by 5, keeping its sign.",
              "work": "$5 \\times (-3) = -15$"
            },
            {
              "desc": "Write the result as a sum.",
              "work": "$= 10x-15$"
            }
          ],
          "answer": "$10x-15$"
        },
        {
          "type": "practiceSet",
          "eyebrow": "Practice",
          "title": "Practice set",
          "subtitle": "Reveal each solution and mark yourself honestly.",
